# Editorial scene proof: scoped read-only review

One linkage gap remains before the semantic receipt can be treated as describing the captured frame: the producer's revision is not matched to the PNG's post-capture state. The source review otherwise supports the intended overlay isolation and exact pixel comparison; no fresh capture has verified the visual result.

The change addresses the known DOM contamination without weakening the exact RGBA gate. The capture helper marks intersecting elements outside the active canvas's ancestor chain, applies `visibility: hidden !important` during the element screenshot, checks that the canvas remains visible, and removes the temporary markers and stylesheet in `finally` (`benches/scripts/support/editorial-scene-capture.mts:13–71`). The current presentation overlays are separate header, controls, telemetry, payload, and viewport status elements (`benches/src/components/presentation-layout.tsx:58–91`; `benches/src/surfaces/benchmark/comparison-workload-viewport.tsx:360–389`), so this mechanism covers the identified live panel and labels. It does not mask any pixel rectangle or change the comparator's zero-difference condition (`benches/scripts/compare-editorial-hero-pixels.mts:154–166`). The `hiddenOverlayElements > 0` receipt requirement is a useful sanity check, although it is not an independent pixel-level proof that every overlay was absent.

The semantic hook is confined to `editorialProof=1` and is requested only for an editorial pixel capture (`comparison-workload.ts:1091–1121`; `run-presentation-workload-probe.mts:466,604–636`). It responds to a fresh nonce, returns errors through the callback, and removes its listener on disposal. It does no per-frame serialization. The producer reads the runtime's current configuration, revision, camera matrices, obstacle matrix/bounds, text properties and flow, committed aggregate measurement, and full line/glyph columns (`editorial-scene-proof.ts:15–109`). `Text.glyphs()` can acquire owned inspection columns in the render planner (`packages/glyph/src/internal/render-planner.ts:923–941`), so querying only after the PNG is the correct order. The captured artifact retains the query order marker and the PNG state; both files are hashed in their receipt. The comparator verifies those file hashes, compares common harness source hashes across arms, keeps the externally pinned archives and distinct roots, and reports line, glyph geometry, and glyph identity differences separately. The pixel result remains the gate.

## Finding to fix before treating a semantic result as the captured frame

The producer's `configuration.revision` is not checked against `editorialPixelCapture.state.configurationRevision`, and its viewport/DPR, camera kind, font fixture, grid, workload, and animation state are not checked against the independently read capture state. `semanticArtifact.capturedState` is a copy of the already stored pixel state; `readCapture` compares that copy back to itself (`run-presentation-workload-probe.mts:616–635`; `compare-editorial-hero-pixels.mts:274–277`). Thus the hash authenticates a later query, but the receipt does not establish that it observed the same committed scene as the PNG. The scene is paused and stable in the intended run, so this is a linkage gap rather than evidence of a present mismatch. Compare producer configuration/revision and camera kind to the post-PNG state in the producer before writing the semantic file, or take both state reads in the same `page.evaluate` call and assert their equality. Keep the semantic query after the screenshot.

## Scope and limits

The producer captures the actual `flow` and positioned line/glyph rows needed to determine whether the remaining body difference is layout or raster coverage. `glyphGeometryRowsDiffer` omits stable ID, font handle, and font slot while `glyphIdentityRowsDiffer` retains them, avoiding a false placement signal from runtime handle identity. No current run of the new capture is included here, so the known outside-panel differences of 33,751 WebGPU and 43,173 WebGL2 pixels remain unexplained. A scene-only rerun must still pass zero different RGBA pixels; matching semantic rows would narrow the investigation to renderer coverage or capture timing, while differing flow/line/geometry rows would identify the first layout divergence. The new semantic artifact is diagnostic evidence, not an independent expected-layout oracle.

No tests, builds, gates, or browser captures were run in this review.
